From: Piotr Kubik <piotr.kubik@adtran.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Jul 2026 08:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: phylink: unwind failed PHY bringup

phylink_bringup_phy() publishes the PHY (pl->phydev, phy->phylink,
phy->phy_link_change) before its last possible failure point, the
phy_eee_rx_clock_stop() register access. When an SFP module is
removed while its PHY is being probed, that access fails with -ENXIO
and the error is returned with the half-attached state left in place.
sfp_sm_probe_phy() then removes and frees the PHY device, while
pl->phydev still points at it. A subsequent phylink_stop() runs
phy_stop() on the freed PHY device:

  sfp sfp1: sfp_add_phy failed: -ENXIO
  sfp sfp1: module removed
  Unable to handle kernel read from unreadable memory at 0x1c0
  pc : _phy_state_machine+0x1bc/0x320
  Call trace:
   _phy_state_machine+0x1bc/0x320 (P)
   phy_stop+0x70/0x160
   phylink_stop+0x38/0x140
   mtk_stop+0x3c/0x470
   mux_poll+0xcc/0x218

Observed with an SFP-10G-T module during fast insert/remove
cycling. Clear the published references on the failure
path, mirroring phylink_disconnect_phy().

--- a/drivers/net/phy/phylink.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/phylink.c
@@ -2529,10 +2529,29 @@ static int phylink_bringup_phy(struct ph
 			ret = 0;
 	}
 
-	if (ret == 0 && phy_interrupt_is_valid(phy))
+	if (ret) {
+		/* Unwind the partial attach: callers dispose of the PHY
+		 * device when this function fails (e.g. sfp_sm_probe_phy()),
+		 * so no stale reference may be left behind here, or a later
+		 * phylink_stop() runs phy_stop() on a freed PHY device.
+		 */
+		mutex_lock(&pl->phydev_mutex);
+		mutex_lock(&phy->lock);
+		mutex_lock(&pl->state_mutex);
+		pl->phydev = NULL;
+		phy->phylink = NULL;
+		phy->phy_link_change = NULL;
+		mutex_unlock(&pl->state_mutex);
+		mutex_unlock(&phy->lock);
+		mutex_unlock(&pl->phydev_mutex);
+
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	if (phy_interrupt_is_valid(phy))
 		phy_request_interrupt(phy);
 
-	return ret;
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static int phylink_attach_phy(struct phylink *pl, struct phy_device *phy,
